;; FH-BEHAVIOR-007 — one node, more than one mechanism: the refs COMPOSE, in a
;; defined order, and every participant is released.
;;
;; A behavior attaches through `cloneElement` with its own ref; the DOM top
;; layer installs its idempotent host call as a ref on the element's props.
;; React holds ONE ref per element, so an element carrying both is not a
;; failure an implementation notices — it is a silent CLOBBER, whose symptom is
;; a popover that never opens or a behavior that never connects. The substrate
;; composes instead.
;;
;; Two shapes of evidence, because the law has two halves that no single tier
;; can see:
;;
;;   THE ORDER is a fact about the composition itself, so it is pinned on the
;;   composition itself, as an exact SEQUENCE rather than a set. Participants
;;   take the node from the INSIDE OUT — the element's own intrinsics, then
;;   each decorating boundary in the order it encloses the node — and release
;;   in the exact reverse. `:nested` proves the rule survives a third
;;   participant, which is the whole point of composing rather than special-
;;   casing a pair. `:protocols` proves a release reaches a participant under
;;   whichever protocol it answered with: a ref that returned a cleanup
;;   function is released by CALLING it, one that returned anything else is
;;   released with nil, exactly as React would have released each.
;;
;;   THE COMBINATION is a real-DOM fact. A structural test cannot see a
;;   clobbered ref — the tree records the behavior boundary and the top-layer
;;   desired state whether or not either mechanism ever reached the node — so
;;   the proof is a mounted element carrying BOTH, where the behavior's own
;;   DOM write and the platform's `:popover-open` state are read back off the
;;   SAME node.
;;
;; `:renders` is how many further commits the combination performs with the
;; desired state unchanged. The composed ref is deliberately fresh at each
;; commit (that re-entry is what makes the top layer's diff against the live
;; node cheap), so React detaches and re-attaches the whole chain every time —
;; and the behavior, whose own ref is deliberately stable, must nevertheless
;; still hold ONE connection with no re-connection churn. More than one commit,
;; so churn would show as a count rather than as a coincidence.
;;
;; `:control` is the mount that makes the absence mean something: the same
;; markup with NEITHER mechanism, measured first, so a zero after teardown is
;; the substrate's release rather than a counter that was never written.
{:fh/id "FH-BEHAVIOR-007"
 :fh/law
 "When more than one mechanism needs a ref on one element the refs COMPOSE rather than clobber: participants receive the node from the inside out — the element's own intrinsics, then each decorating boundary outward — and are released in the exact reverse, under whichever release protocol each answered with. A mounted element carrying both a behavior and a top-layer intrinsic proves both received the same node and both released, measured against a control mount carrying neither."

 ;; One element, two mechanisms.
 :pair
 {:attach  [[:intrinsic :attach] [:decoration :attach]]
  :release [[:decoration :release] [:intrinsic :release]]}

 ;; A third mechanism participates by chaining onto the pair — O(1), not a
 ;; fresh ruling per pair.
 :nested
 {:attach  [[:intrinsic :attach] [:inner :attach] [:outer :attach]]
  :release [[:outer :release] [:inner :release] [:intrinsic :release]]}

 ;; Release honours the protocol each participant answered with.
 :protocols
 {:attach  [[:cleanup-returning :attach] [:nil-detaching :attach]]
  :release [[:nil-detaching :released-with-nil] [:cleanup-returning :cleanup-called]]}

 ;; The mounted combination.
 :combination
 {:menu-id   "composed-menu"
  :target    :composed/menu
  :attribute "data-measured"
  :value     "layout"
  :renders   3}

 :control
 {:connections 0
  :targets     []
  :lifecycle   []
  :pending     0}

 :mounted
 {:connections 1
  :targets     [:composed/menu]
  :lifecycle   [:connect]}

 :after-teardown
 {:connections 0
  :targets     []
  :lifecycle   [:connect :disconnect]
  :pending     0}}
